      "id": "v01-02-c",
      "topic": "Education",
      "difficulty": 2,
      "keyType": "inference",
      "passage": "When the Erasmus programme was launched in 1987, it was designed for students in higher education who wished to spend part of their degree at a university in another participating country. In its first year, around 3,200 students took part. In 2014 it was merged with several other EU schemes for education, training, youth and sport to form Erasmus+. The new programme kept the familiar brand but widened its audience considerably: apprentices, school pupils, teachers, adult learners and youth workers can now receive support, and funding is available for partnerships between organisations as well as for individual mobility. Its budget for 2021–2027 is substantially larger than that of its predecessor.\n\nStudies commissioned by the European Commission report that former participants tend to find employment more quickly than their peers. The authors note, however, that students who choose to go abroad may differ from those who do not in ways that also affect their job prospects.",
