      "topic": "Economic and Monetary Union",
      "difficulty": 3,
      "keyType": "inference",
      "passage": "Before adopting the euro, an EU Member State must meet the convergence criteria. Its inflation rate and long-term interest rates must not exceed those of the three best-performing Member States on price stability by more than 1.5 and 2 percentage points respectively. Its government deficit must not exceed 3% of GDP, and its debt must not exceed 60% of GDP unless the ratio is diminishing sufficiently and approaching that reference value. The country must also have taken part in the Exchange Rate Mechanism (ERM II) for at least two years without severe tensions, and its legislation must be compatible with the Treaties.\n\nDenmark negotiated an opt-out and is not obliged to adopt the euro. The other Member States outside the euro area are legally committed to joining once they meet the criteria, but because participation in ERM II is voluntary, a country can in practice delay adoption by staying out of the mechanism. Croatia became the twentieth member of the euro area in January 2023.",
