      "difficulty": 3,
      "keyType": "combination",
      "passage": "Adopted in 2019, the EU Directive on single-use plastics targets the ten single-use plastic items most often found on European beaches, together with fishing gear. The measures depend on whether sustainable alternatives exist. Where alternatives are readily available and affordable, products such as plastic cutlery, plates, straws and cotton bud sticks were banned from the EU market from July 2021. For products without such alternatives, including tobacco filters and wet wipes, the Directive relies on other tools. Producers of these products must contribute to the costs of waste management and of cleaning up litter, and certain products must carry a label on the plastic they contain and how to dispose of them.\n\nCovered plastic beverage bottles of up to three litres face specific targets: PET bottles must contain at least 25% recycled plastic by 2025, and all such bottles 30% by 2030. Since July 2024, caps and lids have had to remain attached during use on covered beverage containers of up to three litres.",
