      "keyType": "inference",
      "passage": "Most EU legislation is adopted through the ordinary legislative procedure. The European Commission submits a proposal, and the European Parliament and the Council act as co-legislators with equal standing: neither can adopt the act without the other. If they still disagree after the Parliament's second reading, a conciliation committee with an equal number of representatives from each is convened to draft a joint text, which must then be approved by both. If either rejects it, the proposal falls. In practice, most files are now agreed at first reading, often after informal 'trilogue' negotiations between the three institutions. Critics argue that trilogues make it harder for citizens to follow how legislation is shaped, while supporters point to the speed of the agreements reached.\n\nIn the Council, most decisions under the procedure are taken by qualified majority, which requires 55% of Member States representing at least 65% of the EU population. A blocking minority must include at least four Member States.",
