      "difficulty": 2,
      "keyType": "inference",
      "passage": "Around four out of five crop and wildflower species in the EU depend, at least in part, on pollination by animals. Pollinators include not only managed honeybees but also wild bees, hoverflies, butterflies, moths and some beetles, and for several crops wild insects are at least as effective as honeybees. Yet many of these species are in decline: around one in ten bee and butterfly species in Europe is threatened with extinction, and for many others the data are too poor to assess their status. The main drivers include changes in land use, intensive farming, pesticide use, invasive alien species, pathogens and climate change. Because these pressures interact, the loss is difficult to attribute to any single cause.\n\nThe European Commission launched the EU Pollinators Initiative in 2018. A revised initiative adopted in 2023 added actions to restore habitats in farmland and urban areas and to reduce the impact of pesticides, and it set the objective of reversing the decline by 2030.",
