      "difficulty": 2,
      "keyType": "paraphrase",
      "passage": "The Schengen Agreement was signed in 1985 by Belgium, France, Germany, Luxembourg and the Netherlands. It was followed in 1990 by a convention setting out how internal border controls would be abolished and what common rules would compensate for their removal, including a shared visa policy, police cooperation and a common database, the Schengen Information System. The rules were incorporated into EU law by the Treaty of Amsterdam in 1999. The area now includes most EU Member States and four non-EU countries. Member States that wish to join must first be assessed as applying the rules correctly, and their full membership requires a unanimous decision of the Council.\n\nA member may temporarily reintroduce internal border checks as a last resort in the event of a serious threat to public policy or internal security. Such controls must be notified, limited in time and proportionate. Several countries have repeatedly extended controls in recent years, prompting debate about whether temporary measures have become permanent in practice.",
