      "difficulty": 2,
      "keyType": "paraphrase",
      "passage": "On 9 May 1950, French Foreign Minister Robert Schuman proposed placing French and West German coal and steel production under a common High Authority, within an organisation open to other European countries. Coal and steel were the raw materials of armaments, and the aim was to make war between historic rivals 'not merely unthinkable, but materially impossible'. The plan had been prepared largely by Jean Monnet, who became the first president of the High Authority.\n\nThe Treaty of Paris, signed in 1951 by six countries, established the European Coal and Steel Community (ECSC). The United Kingdom was invited to take part but declined, unwilling to transfer control of its industries to a supranational body. Unlike most later treaties, the Treaty of Paris was concluded for a limited period of fifty years; it expired in 2002, and the ECSC's remaining activities and assets were taken over by the European Community. Today, 9 May is celebrated as Europe Day in recognition of Schuman's declaration.",
