      "id": "v01-13-c",
      "topic": "Public health",
      "difficulty": 2,
      "keyType": "paraphrase",
      "passage": "Antimicrobial resistance (AMR) occurs when bacteria, viruses, fungi or parasites change over time and no longer respond to the medicines designed to kill them. It is a natural process, but it is accelerated by the overuse and misuse of antimicrobials in human medicine and animal husbandry. Antibiotics are ineffective against viral infections such as colds and influenza, yet they are still frequently taken for them, sometimes because patients expect a prescription and sometimes because doctors cannot quickly rule out a bacterial infection. Faster diagnostic tests could therefore reduce unnecessary use, although they are not yet available for many conditions.\n\nIn farming, the EU banned antibiotics as growth promoters in animal feed in 2006, and since 2022 it has also restricted their routine preventive use in groups of healthy animals. Developing new antibiotics is scientifically difficult and commercially unattractive, because new drugs that work against resistant bacteria are likely to be held in reserve and sold in small quantities.",
